## Task Tracking

- **Entry protocol**: one state source, one detail location — `.project/INDEX.md` is the single source of truth and carries the Assignee column. Read it and run `python3 scripts/check-project.py` before work; the round's scope comes from the session instruction, never from a shared file — without one, take rows that are `active` and assigned to you and state which rows you will touch; treat only `status: active` documents as execution input
- Never start a task from "pending confirmation / not implemented / deferred" found in `superseded`, `archived`, or `evidence` documents; agent session memory and logs are append-only records, not a status source
- Assign each new task a stable ID in `INDEX.md` with an assignee, closing condition, and evidence path; break large stages into a subtask chain, express the current stage as a group heading, put blockers in the Notes column; one row one owner, and on handover change the assignee plus the break point instead of opening a new task
- Mark a task `done` only when evidence exists on disk and the closing condition is met; deferred, blocked, and disabled are not done. Commit, push, and deploy are separately authorized steps
- The playbook lives in [workflow](.project/rules/workflow.md)

## Development Rules
- Read `INDEX.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` before starting work to catch up on project progress
- Add comments to file headers, core functions, and business logic; use type annotations consistently
- Record any open discussion items in `.project/INDEX.md` under their own ID instead of creating a new list file

## Commit Rules
- Do not commit intermediate artifacts, auxiliary documents, or development plans
- Decide whether a change is user-visible and record it in `CHANGELOG.md`; that file covers functional changes only, never implementation details

## Other
- Put long development guides in `.project/` and link to them here, e.g. `.project/release-note.md`; this file must not exceed 50 lines
